{
  "teamId": 109,
  "year": 2022,
  "narrative": "The 2022 Arizona Diamondbacks were a team caught in perpetual transition, never quite finding the consistency needed to compete in a tough NL West. They opened April with a losing record and a bullpen that was already showing cracks, highlighted by closer Mark Melancon appearing in several of the season's most pivotal moments — often on the wrong side of history. The team's youth was evident, with prospects like Alek Thomas and Geraldo Perdomo getting their feet wet, but the experienced pieces failed to hold the fort long enough for those young players to make a real difference.\n\nMay offered a glimmer of hope, as the Diamondbacks posted their only winning month of the first half with a 15-14 record. Pavin Smith's clutch two-run homer and Daulton Varsho's timely doubles provided brief flashes of offensive life, and the bullpen occasionally delivered, including a double-play ball that saved a tight game. But June unraveled quickly — a 9-16 collapse driven by costly losses in late-game situations and a pitching staff that couldn't protect leads. The dog days of summer in July continued that trend, with a 11-14 record punctuated by crushing homers surrendered by Noé Ramirez that extinguished any rally momentum.\n\nAugust was the season's most confusing chapter — the Diamondbacks actually went 16-12, their best month, yet the biggest individual plays were frequently catastrophic, including Ian Kennedy surrendering a devastating two-run blast to Brandon Crawford that swung nearly a full win in probability. Jake McCarthy emerged as a bright spot, and the young outfield showed signs of life. But September came crashing down at 11-17, with the playoff race long since over and the team sleepwalking through a frustrating finish. Sergio Alcántara's own walk-off homer against his former team summed up the cruel irony of an organization in flux.\n\nThe Diamondbacks finished 74-88, a familiar position for a franchise rebuilding on the fly. Christian Walker's 36 home runs provided a genuine star-level performance, and the young core of Thomas, McCarthy, and Corbin Carroll offered real reasons for optimism heading into the future. But 2022 ultimately served as a reminder that a team cannot outscore a leaky bullpen and a below-average offense night after night — and the gap between potential and production remained painfully wide all season long.",
